// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row method_with_unsized_wrapper_param_not_found (tier 3,
// `refuses`). A LEGAL PROGRAM IS REFUSED: an inherent METHOD whose by-value
// parameter is a smart-pointer struct over a trait object — `r: Rc<dyn Sp>` —
// IS NOT FOUND AT ALL. `h.eat(rc)` reports
//     method call: 'Holder' has no method 'eat'
// on a method that is declared four lines above it.
//
// Measured 2026-09-08 on build 8e5f92705b285d29 43 and again on the binary of
// this commit. TWO CONTROLS on the same binary separate the parameter type from
// every other variable:
//   * the byte-identical method with `r: Rc<A>` (sized payload) IS found, and
//     correctly reports "use of moved variable 'rc'" for the same body — so it
//     is not the receiver, the `pub`, the impl block, or the name.
//   * the same signature as a FREE function, `fn sinkrc(r: Rc<dyn Sp>) -> i64`,
//     IS found and compiles — so it is not the parameter type on its own, it is
//     the parameter type IN AN INHERENT METHOD.
// rustc accepts the program; Rust has no rule that hides a method by its
// parameter type.
//
// Found while enumerating the coercion-consumes-its-source class by POSITION
// (the method-argument position was the one position of eight that could not be
// measured at all, because the method does not exist). It is NOT a member of
// that class — no coercion happens, because resolution fails first.
// ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-09-08 (round 2026-09-08-fatrepr), 15 hand programs on build
// `2c5a33ef99e94fc9 43`. BOTH of the readings above are too narrow, and the
// third one is wrong:
//   * the method DECLARED and never called compiles rc 0 — it IS registered;
//   * the SAME call with the argument already typed `Rc<dyn Sp>` (routed through
//     `let rd: Rc<dyn Sp> = rc;`) compiles and RUNS rc 0 — it IS found;
//   * the TRAIT-method spelling fails identically, so "an inherent method" is
//     not the property; `&Rc<dyn Sp>` fails too, so "by-value" is not either;
//   * `Box<A>` -> `Box<dyn Sp>` and `&A` -> `&dyn Sp` at the SAME method-argument
//     position compile and run.
// THE PROPERTY IS: an argument that must UNSIZE to reach the parameter is
// rejected by the method-candidate SELECTOR before any coercion runs, and the
// rejection is reported as "has no method". The site is
// `SemaChecker::arg_compatible_for_dispatch`, whose own comment states the rule
// ("The selector must accept what the coercion pipeline can produce, or the
// candidate is rejected before the coercion ever runs and the call reports
// 'no method' instead of coercing") and then implements it for `&[E;N]` ->
// `&[E]` alone. The wrong SENTENCE is a class, not this program: an ill-typed
// `W<bool>` at a `W<i64>` parameter prints "has no method" too.
// The program is UNCHANGED; only the reading is.
package method_with_unsized_wrapper_param_not_found;
use logos.lang.rc;

trait Sp { fn v(&self) -> i64; }
struct A { x: i64 }
impl Sp for A { fn v(&self) -> i64 { return self.x; } }

struct Holder { n: i64 }
impl Holder { pub fn eat(&self, r: Rc<dyn Sp>) -> i64 { return r.v() + self.n; } }

fn main() -> i64 {
    let h: Holder = Holder { n: 1i64 };
    let rc: Rc<A> = rc_new(A { x: 17i64 });
    if h.eat(rc) != 18i64 { return 1i64; }
    return 0i64;
}
